### SWEEP — 2 structures x 1 sharing rates, 182 requests per cell (60s steady state)

  zero            share 0.00  oracle  0.000  measured  0.011  ttft_p50      77.7  delta       0.3  OK
  system          share 0.50  oracle  0.123  measured  0.137  ttft_p50      72.1  delta      -4.8  OK

### WHAT THE CACHE COSTS WHEN IT NEVER HELPS (the mandatory control, §7)

  zero sharing: block hit rate 0.0110 (expected ~0). Turning the cache on COSTS 0.32 ms at TTFT p50 and 3.16 ms at p99, for zero benefit.
    That is the honest floor: radix walk, insert and refcount bookkeeping on every request with nothing to reuse (§7). It is published here whether or not it flatters, and if it is material it is an argument for making the cache adaptive.

### HIT RATE AND TTFT vs SHARING RATE, BY STRUCTURE

structure  share  oracle  hit_rate  req_hits  depth  evict  cow  shared>1  ttft_p50  ttft_p99  d_p50  d_p99    n  valid
---------  -----  ------  --------  --------  -----  -----  ---  --------  --------  --------  -----  -----  ---  -----
     zero   0.00   0.000     0.011     0.995   0.99      0    0         0      77.7     165.2    0.3    3.2  118     OK
   system   0.50   0.123     0.137     1.000  12.35      0    0         0      72.1     124.7   -4.8  -38.1  118     OK

  oracle    = generator's infinite-cache block sharing rate, the UPPER BOUND.
  hit_rate  = server blocks_reused / blocks_required over THIS window only.
  depth     = mean partial-hit depth in blocks, over this window.
  d_p50/99  = cache-on minus cache-off TTFT; NEGATIVE is the cache helping.
  Percentiles come from the artifacts' raw ttft_ms samples at print time,
  never from a stored scalar (methodology §5, R15).

  A hit rate is meaningless without the structure beside it (§7): the
  conversational rows are the MOST FAVOURABLE shape for a radix cache and
  their numbers are not comparable to the system-prompt rows.

### BREAK-EVEN SHARING RATE — where the cache starts paying for itself

  system          only 1 usable point(s); a crossover needs at least 2

  Read this as a curve, not a point. A TTFT improvement quoted at one
  sharing rate is a choice of workload; the rate at which the sign flips is
  the number that survives a follow-up question.

